  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nTonight through Friday...\n\nA subtle vort max will continue to lift through the region tonight, \nas southwesterly midlevel winds pull warm moist air into the region. \nA few scattered showers will be possible, with the best chance over \nsoutheast Nebraska early Thursday morning. Otherwise, the \ncombination of light surface winds and cool temperatures within\nthe moist airmass has yielded more expansive development of fog\nthis evening. Latest RRFS and HREF runs suggest a few locations\ncould see visibilities drop under 1/4 SM, from eastern Nebraska\nthrough south central Iowa. Thus, a Dense Fog Advisory has been\nissued for tonight through Thursday morning. \n\nVisibilities will improve by late Thursday morning as winds pick up \nand surface temperatures rise. Afternoon highs are currently \nforecast to reach the low to mid 70s in northeast Nebraska, with low \nto mid 80s expected over far southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa. \n\nAnother weak shortwave will spill into the forecast area Thursday \nafternoon, bringing another round of wet weather, this time to \nnortheast Nebraska, Thursday night into Friday morning. \n\nTemperatures will rise into the mid to upper 80s Friday, allowing \n1000-2000 J/kg of instability to pool over the region. A cold front \nwill sweep through the region Friday evening, bringing at least a \nchance for strong to severe storms. Similar to the event we had on \nMonday, forecast hodographs feature a notable amount of turning in \nthe low levels. However, deep layer shear looks fairly marginal, at \naround 20-30 kts, with better shear trailing behind the front. \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nBy early Saturday morning, the cold front should push off to our \nsoutheast. A few more showers and storms will be possible along\nthe front early Saturday morning before it exits the forecast \narea. Behind the front, most areas reach the low to mid 70s, \nwith a few of our southern counties reaching the low 80s.\n\nSunday will see a more substantial wave eject from Colorado toward \nthe area. The feature will help trigger or next round for rainfall, \nparticularly across the southern half of the forecast area. Periodic \nchances for more rainfall continue through Thursday as we see \nmore disturbances traverse the region, but chances remain low \nat 15 to 30%.",
